From 6233a36b37a448674164f39d68a09f75577a27d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:11:18 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] lib/streamaggr: keep output entries in sharded maps
 instead of a sync.Map

A heap profile of vmagent aggregating one 30k-key deployment -- about nine
million output series -- put 1058 MiB of its 3136 MiB in sync.Map's own trie
nodes (newIndirectNode and newEntryNode), roughly 120 bytes of structure per
series on top of the entry it points at. A plain map slot costs a few dozen.

The entries now live in 256 maps, each behind its own RWMutex, chosen by an
xxhash of the output key. A push looks the key up under the read lock and, on a
miss, looks again under the write lock before inserting, so two pushers
creating the same entry at once still end up on one. A flush copies one shard's
entries out under the read lock and flushes them with the lock released, so it
never holds up pushes to a shard for the length of a flush. A stale entry is
removed from its shard as soon as it is marked, as before: a push that finds it
marked goes back to look it up again, and would keep doing so while it stayed.

On ACG-shaped series (ten labels, four of them UUIDs) with the production
aggregation config, per output series:

  heap      434 -> 360 bytes  (-17%)
  objects   8.6 -> 6.2

BenchmarkAggregatorsPushACGShape is unchanged within noise (sum_samples_total
124 -> 126 ms, sum_samples 120 -> 125 ms median of six, ranges overlapping).

TestAggrOutputsConserveSamplesUnderConcurrency pushes from eight goroutines onto
overlapping series with a flush running throughout and checks that what is
flushed adds up to what was pushed; TestAggrOutputsDropStaleEntriesAndRebuild
checks a stale entry leaves its shard and a later push rebuilds it. Both fail
when the re-check before insert is removed (1 to 10 samples lost out of
240000), when the last flush leaves entries behind, or when a stale entry is
only marked.
